response:
 The center of the smaller circle lies on the $x$-axis since the two circles are symmetric about the $x$-axis. Let $C$ be the center of the smaller circle. Let the point of intersection of the $y$-axis and the ellipse be $P=(0,\sqrt{6/5})$. We have \[ \tan(\angle{OCP}) = \tan\left(\angle{POC}-\angle{PCO}\right)=\frac{\sin\angle{POC}-\sin\angle{PCO}}{\cos\angle{POC}+\cos\angle{PCO}}
=\frac{\frac{OC}{OC}-\frac{OC}{OC}}{1+1\cdot \frac{OC}{OC}}=0, \] so $\angle{OCP}=0$, i.e. $OC=OP=\sqrt{\frac{6}{5}}$.

Therefore, $r=OC-\frac{OC}{2}=\boxed{\sqrt{\frac{6}{5}}}-\frac{\sqrt{\frac{6}{5}}}{2}=\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{6}{5}}$. </think> <answer> \frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\frac{6}{5}} </answer>

