Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \tanh \left(\frac{11x}{2} + 3\right)$. The inverse of the function $f(x)$ is denoted by $f^{-1}(x)$ and can be found as follows:

Given $f(x) = \tanh \left(\frac{11x}{2} + 3\right)$, to find $f^{-1}(x)$, we set $y = \tanh \left(\frac{11x}{2} + 3\right)$ and solve for $x$ in terms of $y$:

\begin{align*}
y &= \tanh \left(\frac{11x}{2} + 3\right) \\
\tanh^{-1}(y) &= \frac{11x}{2} + 3 \\
\Rightarrow \quad x &= \frac{2 \cdot \tanh^{-1}(y) - 6}{11}
\end{align*}

Hence, the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ is:

$$f^{-1}(x) = \frac{2 \tanh^{-1}(x) - 6}{11}$$

The domain of the inverse function is the range of the original function, which for the hyperbolic tangent is $(-1,1)$, and the range is the entire real line, since the inverse hypersul tangent covers all possible real values. Therefore, the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $-1 < x < 1$.

